# Backend architecture

Canonical: `docs/Personal_Rules.md` section 12.

## Layer responsibilities

- Controllers translate HTTP input/output only. They call services and contain no business decisions, TypeORM queries, or transaction logic.
- Services own business rules, business-data authorization, workflow orchestration, and transaction boundaries.
- Repositories own persistence. Controllers do not import TypeORM infrastructure.
- Services do not import TypeORM Repository/query builders or execute queries. Only the approved transaction runner may be used to define boundaries; services do not query through its context.
- Repository methods express application intent, such as `findActiveTaskById`. Do not expose unrestricted generic CRUD or query builders to higher layers.
- Entities stay in persistence. Repositories return the data services need; controllers return response DTOs.

## Modules and dependencies

- Register each business feature through a NestJS module. Other features use exported services/contracts rather than its repositories/entities.
- Use constructor injection through Nest providers; no manual construction of services/repositories or mutable global state.
- Dependencies run controllers → services → repositories. Avoid circular modules; do not use `forwardRef()` as a routine solution. Correct the boundary or extract shared responsibility.

- Life-simulator rules/calculations and confirmed save/load workflows belong in NestJS services; repositories own SQL Server access. Electron main/preload and React components do not become a second game-rules or persistence layer. Confirm game-time behaviour before implementing it; technical/audit timestamps remain Adelaide time.
